They are different fields, but the borrow checker never looks inside `log_reorder`. It only sees the signature `fn log_reorder(&mut self, ...)`, and that says "I may change *anything* in `self`", including `self.items`, which the loop is still iterating. Rust checks each function against the signatures of the functions it calls, never their bodies, so a change inside a method can't break its callers.

Disjoint field borrows do work, but only when the fields are named directly in the same function. You can fix it in three ways:

**1. Touch the fields directly** (the smallest change):

```rust
pub fn check_reorders(&mut self) {
    for item in &self.items {
        if item.qty < item.reorder_level {
            // borrowck sees `self.items` (shared) and `self.reorder_log` (mutable): no overlap
            self.reorder_log.push(item.sku.clone());
            self.pending_orders += 1;
        }
    }
}
```

**2. Collect first, mutate afterwards.** Use this when the helper really needs all of `&mut self`:

```rust
pub fn check_reorders(&mut self) {
    let low: Vec<String> = self
        .items
        .iter()
        .filter(|item| item.qty < item.reorder_level)
        .map(|item| item.sku.clone())
        .collect();

    for sku in &low {
        self.log_reorder(sku);
    }
}
```

**3. Give the log its own type.** I'd do this if the log grows more logic:

```rust
#[derive(Default)]
pub struct ReorderLog {
    entries: Vec<String>,
    pending: u32,
}

impl ReorderLog {
    fn record(&mut self, sku: &str) {
        self.entries.push(sku.to_string());
        self.pending += 1;
    }
}

pub struct Warehouse {
    items: Vec<Item>,
    reorders: ReorderLog,
}

impl Warehouse {
    pub fn check_reorders(&mut self) {
        for item in &self.items {
            if item.qty < item.reorder_level {
                self.reorders.record(&item.sku); // borrows only `self.reorders`
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Option 3 states the real dependency in the type: recording a reorder needs the log, not the whole warehouse. Then the borrow checker can see that the two borrows don't overlap.

The usual safe approach is a doubly linked list whose nodes live in a `Vec` and point to each other by **index** instead of by pointer. A `HashMap` maps each key to its slot. That gives you O(1) everywhere without `unsafe`, without `Rc<RefCell<..>>` cycles, and with good cache locality. Since the capacity is fixed, a full cache just reuses the slot of the evicted entry, so the `Vec` never needs holes.

```rust
use std::borrow::Borrow;
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::hash::Hash;

struct Node<K, V> {
    key: K,
    value: V,
    prev: Option<usize>,
    next: Option<usize>,
}

/// Least-recently-used cache with O(1) `get` and `put`.
///
/// Entries are stored in `nodes` and linked by index into a doubly linked list
/// (head = most recently used, tail = least recently used).
pub struct LruCache<K, V> {
    capacity: usize,
    map: HashMap<K, usize>,
    nodes: Vec<Node<K, V>>,
    head: Option<usize>,
    tail: Option<usize>,
}

impl<K: Hash + Eq + Clone, V> LruCache<K, V> {
    pub fn new(capacity: usize) -> Self {
        assert!(capacity > 0, "capacity must be at least 1");
        Self {
            capacity,
            map: HashMap::with_capacity(capacity),
            nodes: Vec::with_capacity(capacity),
            head: None,
            tail: None,
        }
    }

    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {
        self.nodes.len()
    }

    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
        self.nodes.is_empty()
    }

    /// Returns the value and marks the entry as most recently used.
    pub fn get<Q>(&mut self, key: &Q) -> Option<&V>
    where
        K: Borrow<Q>,
        Q: Hash + Eq + ?Sized,
    {
        let idx = *self.map.get(key)?;
        self.move_to_front(idx);
        Some(&self.nodes[idx].value)
    }

    /// Inserts or updates an entry. Returns the evicted entry, if one was evicted.
    pub fn put(&mut self, key: K, value: V) -> Option<(K, V)> {
        if let Some(&idx) = self.map.get(&key) {
            self.nodes[idx].value = value;
            self.move_to_front(idx);
            return None;
        }

        if self.nodes.len() < self.capacity {
            let idx = self.nodes.len();
            self.nodes.push(Node { key: key.clone(), value, prev: None, next: None });
            self.map.insert(key, idx);
            self.push_front(idx);
            return None;
        }

        // Full: reuse the slot of the least recently used entry.
        let idx = self.tail.expect("a full cache has a tail");
        self.unlink(idx);
        let old_key = std::mem::replace(&mut self.nodes[idx].key, key.clone());
        let old_value = std::mem::replace(&mut self.nodes[idx].value, value);
        self.map.remove(&old_key);
        self.map.insert(key, idx);
        self.push_front(idx);
        Some((old_key, old_value))
    }

    fn unlink(&mut self, idx: usize) {
        let (prev, next) = (self.nodes[idx].prev, self.nodes[idx].next);
        match prev {
            Some(p) => self.nodes[p].next = next,
            None => self.head = next,
        }
        match next {
            Some(n) => self.nodes[n].prev = prev,
            None => self.tail = prev,
        }
    }

    fn push_front(&mut self, idx: usize) {
        self.nodes[idx].prev = None;
        self.nodes[idx].next = self.head;
        match self.head {
            Some(h) => self.nodes[h].prev = Some(idx),
            None => self.tail = Some(idx),
        }
        self.head = Some(idx);
    }

    fn move_to_front(&mut self, idx: usize) {
        if self.head != Some(idx) {
            self.unlink(idx);
            self.push_front(idx);
        }
    }
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::LruCache;

    #[test]
    fn evicts_least_recently_used() {
        let mut cache = LruCache::new(2);
        cache.put("a".to_string(), 1);
        cache.put("b".to_string(), 2);
        assert_eq!(cache.get("a"), Some(&1)); // "a" is now the most recent
        assert_eq!(cache.put("c".to_string(), 3), Some(("b".to_string(), 2)));
        assert_eq!(cache.get("b"), None);
        assert_eq!(cache.get("c"), Some(&3));
    }

    #[test]
    fn updating_a_key_refreshes_it() {
        let mut cache = LruCache::new(2);
        cache.put(1, "one");
        cache.put(2, "two");
        cache.put(1, "uno");
        cache.put(3, "three"); // evicts 2, not 1
        assert_eq!(cache.get(&1), Some(&"uno"));
        assert_eq!(cache.get(&2), None);
        assert_eq!(cache.len(), 2);
    }

    #[test]
    fn capacity_one() {
        let mut cache = LruCache::new(1);
        cache.put('x', 10);
        cache.put('y', 20);
        assert_eq!(cache.get(&'x'), None);
        assert_eq!(cache.get(&'y'), Some(&20));
    }
}
```

Some notes for the write-up:

- **Complexity:** `get` is a hash lookup plus a few index updates. `put` is the same, plus at most one `remove` and one `insert` in the map. Both are O(1) on average.
- **Why `K: Clone`:** the key is stored twice, once in the map and once in the node, because eviction needs to know which map entry to remove. If cloning is expensive, store `Rc<K>` in both places.
- **`get` takes `&mut self`:** a read changes the recency order. That is inherent to LRU. If you need `&self` reads, you'd use `RefCell` internally, but for an assignment `&mut self` is the honest signature.
- **`Borrow<Q>` on `get`:** it lets you call `cache.get("a")` on a `LruCache<String, _>` without allocating a `String`, the same way `HashMap::get` works.

