The `Arc<Mutex<..>>` part is fine. The problem is **when** you hold the lock.

`tokio::spawn` may move a task to another worker thread at any `.await`. Everything that is alive across an `.await` becomes part of the future's state, so it all has to be `Send`. A `std::sync::MutexGuard` is not `Send`: a std mutex must be unlocked by the thread that locked it. You take the guard on line 20 and keep it across the `.await` on line 21, so the whole future is not `Send`.

Even if it compiled, it would do the wrong thing. Every task would hold the lock for the entire HTTP request, so your "parallel" fetches would run one at a time.

Await first, then lock only for the update:

```rust
handles.push(tokio::spawn(async move {
    let body = fetch(&url).await;
    let mut map = counts.lock().unwrap(); // no .await after this point
    *map.entry(url).or_insert(0) += body.len() as u64;
}));
```

The guard is dropped at the end of the block, and no `.await` comes after it, so the future is `Send` again.

Better still, you don't need shared state at all. Let each task return its result and collect them in one place:

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use tokio::task::JoinSet;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let urls = vec![
        "https://example.com/a".to_string(),
        "https://example.com/b".to_string(),
    ];

    let mut tasks = JoinSet::new();
    for url in urls {
        tasks.spawn(async move {
            let body = fetch(&url).await;
            (url, body.len() as u64)
        });
    }

    let mut counts: HashMap<String, u64> = HashMap::new();
    while let Some(res) = tasks.join_next().await {
        let (url, len) = res.expect("fetch task panicked");
        *counts.entry(url).or_insert(0) += len;
    }
    println!("{counts:?}");
}
```

When you really do have to hold a lock across an `.await` (rare), use `tokio::sync::Mutex`. Its guard is `Send`, and you lock it with `counts.lock().await`. For short critical sections like this one, `std::sync::Mutex` is the faster choice. The rule is simply: never keep its guard alive across an await.

Can you refactor this so it doesn't panic everywhere? It's a small tool that reads CSV exports from our greenhouse controllers. When an export is broken I want an error that says which line is wrong, not a panic. thiserror is fine as a dependency.

```rust
use std::fs;

#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct Reading {
    pub sensor_id: String,
    pub timestamp: i64,
    pub temp_c: f32,
    pub humidity: f32,
}

pub fn load_readings(path: &str) -> Vec<Reading> {
    let text = fs::read_to_string(path).unwrap();
    let mut out = Vec::new();
    for line in text.lines().skip(1) {
        if line.trim().is_empty() {
            continue;
        }
        let cols: Vec<&str> = line.split(',').collect();
        let reading = Reading {
            sensor_id: cols[0].to_string(),
            timestamp: cols[1].parse().unwrap(),
            temp_c: cols[2].parse().unwrap(),
            humidity: cols[3].parse().unwrap(),
        };
        if reading.humidity > 100.0 {
            panic!("bad humidity");
        }
        out.push(reading);
    }
    out
}
```

Here's a version with one error enum. Every variant carries the line number, and the parse errors keep their original cause as `source`:

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::num::{ParseFloatError, ParseIntError};
use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};

use thiserror::Error;

#[derive(Debug, Error)]
pub enum LoadError {
    #[error("cannot read {}", .path.display())]
    Io { path: PathBuf, source: std::io::Error },

    #[error("line {line}: expected 4 columns, found {found}")]
    ColumnCount { line: usize, found: usize },

    #[error("line {line}: invalid timestamp {value:?}")]
    Timestamp { line: usize, value: String, source: ParseIntError },

    #[error("line {line}: invalid {field} {value:?}")]
    Number { line: usize, field: &'static str, value: String, source: ParseFloatError },

    #[error("line {line}: humidity {value}% is outside 0-100")]
    HumidityRange { line: usize, value: f32 },
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]
pub struct Reading {
    pub sensor_id: String,
    pub timestamp: i64,
    pub temp_c: f32,
    pub humidity: f32,
}

pub fn load_readings(path: impl AsRef<Path>) -> Result<Vec<Reading>, LoadError> {
    let path = path.as_ref();
    let text = fs::read_to_string(path).map_err(|source| LoadError::Io {
        path: path.to_path_buf(),
        source,
    })?;

    text.lines()
        .enumerate()
        .skip(1) // header
        .filter(|(_, l)| !l.trim().is_empty())
        .map(|(i, l)| parse_line(i + 1, l))
        .collect()
}

fn parse_line(line: usize, raw: &str) -> Result<Reading, LoadError> {
    let cols: Vec<&str> = raw.split(',').map(str::trim).collect();
    let [sensor_id, ts, temp, hum] = cols[..] else {
        return Err(LoadError::ColumnCount { line, found: cols.len() });
    };

    let timestamp = ts.parse::<i64>().map_err(|source| LoadError::Timestamp {
        line,
        value: ts.to_string(),
        source,
    })?;
    let temp_c = parse_f32(line, "temperature", temp)?;
    let humidity = parse_f32(line, "humidity", hum)?;
    if !(0.0..=100.0).contains(&humidity) {
        return Err(LoadError::HumidityRange { line, value: humidity });
    }

    Ok(Reading { sensor_id: sensor_id.to_string(), timestamp, temp_c, humidity })
}

fn parse_f32(line: usize, field: &'static str, value: &str) -> Result<f32, LoadError> {
    value.parse().map_err(|source| LoadError::Number {
        line,
        field,
        value: value.to_string(),
        source,
    })
}
```

