`forEach` ignores what its callback returns. Your async callback returns a promise, `forEach` throws it away and returns right away, and `console.log` runs before any `fetch` has finished.

Use `map` to get the promises and await them all:

```js
async function checkLinks(urls) {
  const settled = await Promise.allSettled(
    urls.map(async (url) => {
      const res = await fetch(url, { method: "HEAD" });
      return { url, ok: res.ok, status: res.status };
    }),
  );

  return settled.map((r, i) =>
    r.status === "fulfilled" ? r.value : { url: urls[i], ok: false, error: r.reason.message },
  );
}
```

I used `allSettled` rather than `all` because `fetch` *rejects* on DNS or connection errors. With `Promise.all`, one dead host would throw away every other result.

If you'd rather check one URL at a time (to be polite to a single server), a plain `for...of` loop works with `await`:

```js
for (const url of urls) {
  const res = await fetch(url, { method: "HEAD" });
  results.push({ url, ok: res.ok });
}
```

Two parts: a small generic `useDebouncedValue` hook, and the component. The component fetches when the *debounced* query changes, and cancels the previous request so that a slow old response can't overwrite a newer one.

```tsx
// useDebouncedValue.ts
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

export function useDebouncedValue<T>(value: T, delayMs = 300): T {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);

  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delayMs);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
  }, [value, delayMs]);

  return debounced;
}
```

```tsx
// RecipeSearch.tsx
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";
import { useDebouncedValue } from "./useDebouncedValue";

type Recipe = { id: string; title: string; minutes: number };

type SearchState =
  | { status: "idle" }
  | { status: "loading" }
  | { status: "done"; recipes: Recipe[] }
  | { status: "error"; message: string };

const MIN_QUERY_LENGTH = 2;

export function RecipeSearch() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");
  const debounced = useDebouncedValue(query.trim(), 300);
  const [state, setState] = useState<SearchState>({ status: "idle" });

  useEffect(() => {
    if (debounced.length < MIN_QUERY_LENGTH) {
      setState({ status: "idle" });
      return;
    }

    const controller = new AbortController();
    setState({ status: "loading" });

    fetch(`/api/recipes?q=${encodeURIComponent(debounced)}`, { signal: controller.signal })
      .then((res) => {
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Search failed (${res.status})`);
        return res.json() as Promise<Recipe[]>;
      })
      .then((recipes) => setState({ status: "done", recipes }))
      .catch((err: unknown) => {
        if (err instanceof DOMException && err.name === "AbortError") return;
        setState({
          status: "error",
          message: err instanceof Error ? err.message : "Search failed",
        });
      });

    return () => controller.abort();
  }, [debounced]);

  return (
    <div className="recipe-search">
      <label htmlFor="recipe-q">Search recipes</label>
      <input
        id="recipe-q"
        type="search"
        value={query}
        onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="e.g. lentil soup"
        autoComplete="off"
      />

      <div aria-live="polite">
        {state.status === "loading" && <p className="muted">Searching…</p>}
        {state.status === "error" && <p role="alert">{state.message}</p>}
        {state.status === "done" && state.recipes.length === 0 && (
          <p>No recipes found for “{debounced}”.</p>
        )}
      </div>

      {state.status === "done" && state.recipes.length > 0 && (
        <ul className="recipe-results">
          {state.recipes.map((r) => (
            <li key={r.id}>
              <a href={`/recipes/${r.id}`}>{r.title}</a>
              <span className="muted"> · {r.minutes} min</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

Design choices:

- **One `SearchState` union instead of `loading`/`error`/`results` booleans.** Impossible combinations like "loading and error at the same time" can't happen, and TypeScript only lets you read `recipes` in the `done` state.
- **The debounce is on the value, not on the handler.** The input stays fully controlled and responsive, and only the request waits. 300 ms feels instant to most people. Go up to 400–500 ms if your API is expensive.
- **Abort on cleanup.** When the user types "lentil" and then "lentil soup", the first request is cancelled. Without that, responses can arrive out of order and show results for the old query.
- **`aria-live="polite"`** makes screen readers announce "Searching…" and "No recipes found" without stealing focus from the input.
- **The minimum length is 2 characters.** It avoids a request for every single letter, which mostly returns half your database anyway.

The cost to the bundle is about 60 lines of your own code, with no dependencies.

Describe the events once as a map from name to payload type, and make the emitter generic over that map. Each method then picks the payload type with `Events[K]`.

```ts
type Listener<T> = (payload: T) => void;

export class TypedEmitter<Events extends Record<string, unknown>> {
  private listeners: { [K in keyof Events]?: Set<Listener<Events[K]>> } = {};

  on<K extends keyof Events>(event: K, listener: Listener<Events[K]>): () => void {
    const set: Set<Listener<Events[K]>> = this.listeners[event] ?? new Set();
    set.add(listener);
    this.listeners[event] = set;
    return () => this.off(event, listener);
  }

  once<K extends keyof Events>(event: K, listener: Listener<Events[K]>): () => void {
    const off = this.on(event, (payload) => {
      off();
      listener(payload);
    });
    return off;
  }

  off<K extends keyof Events>(event: K, listener: Listener<Events[K]>): void {
    this.listeners[event]?.delete(listener);
  }

  emit<K extends keyof Events>(event: K, payload: Events[K]): void {
    // copy first, so listeners that unsubscribe during emit don't break iteration
    for (const listener of [...(this.listeners[event] ?? [])]) {
      listener(payload);
    }
  }
}
```

Your event map:

```ts
export type AppEvents = {
  login: { userId: string; method: "password" | "sso" };
  logout: { userId: string };
  "sync:progress": { done: number; total: number };
  "theme:changed": "light" | "dark";
};

export const bus = new TypedEmitter<AppEvents>();
```

What you get:

```ts
const stop = bus.on("sync:progress", ({ done, total }) => {
  statusBar.text = `${Math.round((done / total) * 100)} %`; // done, total: number
});

bus.emit("login", { userId: "u_42", method: "sso" });  // ok
bus.emit("login", { userId: "u_42" });                 // error: property 'method' is missing
bus.emit("theme:changed", "blue");                     // error: '"blue"' is not assignable
bus.on("logn", () => {});                              // error: '"logn"' is not assignable to keyof AppEvents

stop(); // unsubscribe
```

Two things that trip people up:

1. **Use a `type`, not an `interface`, for the event map.** Interfaces don't get an implicit index signature, so `interface AppEvents { ... }` fails the `Record<string, unknown>` constraint with a confusing error. If you must use an interface, loosen the constraint to `Events extends object`.
2. **Events without a payload.** With this version you'd write `ready: undefined` and call `bus.emit("ready", undefined)`. If that bothers you, make the payload argument optional for those events:

   ```ts
   emit<K extends keyof Events>(
     event: K,
     ...args: Events[K] extends undefined ? [] : [payload: Events[K]]
   ): void {
     const payload = args[0] as Events[K];
     for (const listener of [...(this.listeners[event] ?? [])]) listener(payload);
   }
   ```

   Then `bus.emit("ready")` compiles, and `bus.emit("login")` doesn't.

For Electron IPC between the main and renderer processes, you can reuse the same `AppEvents` map to type `ipcRenderer.on` and `webContents.send` wrappers. Then both sides of the bridge agree on the payloads.

