  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`orders: HTTP ${res.status}`);
  const orders = await res.json();
  for (const order of orders.filter((o) => o.status === "shipped")) {
    await sendInvoice(path.join(outDir, `${order.id}.pdf`), order.email);
  }
});
```

What changes in ESM:

| CommonJS | ESM |
|---|---|
| `require('./mailer')` | `import ... from "./mailer.js"` (the extension is **required**) |
| `module.exports = { sendInvoice }` | `export async function sendInvoice(...)` in `mailer.js` |
| `__dirname` | `import.meta.dirname` (Node 20.11+), or `path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))` on older versions |
| `require('./config.json')` | `import ... with { type: "json" }` |

On Node 20, the JSON import prints an `ExperimentalWarning`. If that bothers you in the logs, read the file instead:

```js
const config = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(new URL("./config.json", import.meta.url), "utf8"));
```

`mailer.js` and every other local `.js` file have to be converted as well, because the `"type"` field applies to all of them. CommonJS packages from npm (like `node-cron`) still work with `import x from "pkg"`.

For a small bot like this I'd take Option A. Use B if you're starting to depend on more ESM-only packages anyway.

