can you make this more modern? Java 17. It's from our library management system, it groups overdue loans by member name with the titles sorted

```java
public Map<String, List<String>> overdueTitlesByMember(List<Loan> loans, LocalDate today) {
    Map<String, List<String>> result = new HashMap<String, List<String>>();
    for (int i = 0; i < loans.size(); i++) {
        Loan loan = loans.get(i);
        if (loan.getReturnedOn() == null && loan.getDueOn().isBefore(today)) {
            String member = loan.getMember().getName();
            if (!result.containsKey(member)) {
                result.put(member, new ArrayList<String>());
            }
            result.get(member).add(loan.getBook().getTitle());
        }
    }
    for (String key : result.keySet()) {
        Collections.sort(result.get(key));
    }
    return result;
}
```

First, move the "is it overdue?" rule into `Loan`, where it belongs and where other code can reuse it:

```java
// in Loan
public boolean isOverdue(LocalDate today) {
    return returnedOn == null && dueOn.isBefore(today);
}
```

Then the method becomes one stream pipeline:

```java
public Map<String, List<String>> overdueTitlesByMember(List<Loan> loans, LocalDate today) {
    return loans.stream()
            .filter(loan -> loan.isOverdue(today))
            .sorted(Comparator.comparing(loan -> loan.getBook().getTitle()))
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                    loan -> loan.getMember().getName(),
                    TreeMap::new,
                    Collectors.mapping(loan -> loan.getBook().getTitle(), Collectors.toList())));
}
```

How it works:

- **Sorting before grouping** gives you sorted title lists for free, because `groupingBy` keeps the encounter order inside each group. You don't need a second loop.
- **`TreeMap::new`** sorts the members alphabetically as well. Your `HashMap` returned them in random order, which tends to look like a bug in reports. Drop that argument if you don't care.
- **No more `containsKey` / `put` / `get` dance.** `groupingBy` creates each member's list the first time it sees that member.

One thing I'd change beyond style: **grouping by name merges different people.** Two members called "Anna Schmidt" end up in one list. If this feeds the reminder letters, group by the member itself (or its id) and let the caller format the name:

```java
public record OverdueLoans(Member member, List<Book> books) {}

public List<OverdueLoans> overdueByMember(List<Loan> loans, LocalDate today) {
    return loans.stream()
            .filter(loan -> loan.isOverdue(today))
            .sorted(Comparator.comparing(loan -> loan.getBook().getTitle()))
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Loan::getMember, LinkedHashMap::new,
                    Collectors.mapping(Loan::getBook, Collectors.toList())))
            .entrySet().stream()
            .map(e -> new OverdueLoans(e.getKey(), e.getValue()))
            .sorted(Comparator.comparing(o -> o.member().getName()))
            .toList();
}
```

That relies on `Member` having a proper `equals`/`hashCode` (by id, if it's a JPA entity). `Stream.toList()` is Java 16+, and it returns an unmodifiable list.

