should I just turn open-in-view back on?

No, keep it off. Turning it off was right. It only *hid* the problem: with open-in-view on, every `a.getBooks().size()` in the controller ran an extra query, on a connection held open for the whole HTTP request. That's an N+1 query pattern: 1 query for the authors plus 1 per author.

What happens now: `findAll()` returns entities, the transaction ends when the service method returns, and the controller touches `books`, a lazy collection with no session left to load it.

The fix is to **load what the endpoint needs inside the transaction, in as few queries as possible**. For a summary with a count, don't load the books at all. Let the database count:

```java
public record AuthorSummary(Long id, String name, Long bookCount) {}

public interface AuthorRepository extends JpaRepository<Author, Long> {

    @Query("""
            select new com.example.library.web.AuthorSummary(a.id, a.name, count(b))
            from Author a left join a.books b
            group by a.id, a.name
            order by a.name
            """)
    List<AuthorSummary> findSummaries();

    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = "books")
    Optional<Author> findWithBooksById(Long id);
}
```

```java
@Service
public class AuthorService {
    private final AuthorRepository authors;

    public AuthorService(AuthorRepository authors) {
        this.authors = authors;
    }

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public List<AuthorSummary> summaries() {
        return authors.findSummaries(); // one query, no entities
    }

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public AuthorDetails details(long id) {
        Author author = authors.findWithBooksById(id)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new AuthorNotFoundException(id));
        return AuthorDetails.from(author); // map while the session is still open
    }
}
```

```java
@GetMapping
public List<AuthorSummary> list() {
    return service.summaries();
}
```

The general rules that keep `LazyInitializationException` away:

1. **Services return DTOs, not entities**, and do the mapping inside the `@Transactional` method. The controller never sees anything lazy.
2. **Every use case states what it fetches**: a constructor-expression query for read-only lists, `@EntityGraph` or `join fetch` when you need the entity with its associations.
3. **Don't "fix" it with `FetchType.EAGER`.** That loads books for *every* author query in the whole application, including the ones that don't need them.

To see the difference, turn on `spring.jpa.show-sql=true` (or better, `logging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=debug`) and compare: the old version with open-in-view ran 1 + N selects, `findSummaries()` runs exactly one.

C program for a little calculator, sometimes prints garbage for the tokens and sometimes works. gcc gives a warning but it compiles so it should be fine??

```
$ gcc -Wall -O2 -o calc calc.c lexer.c
lexer.c: In function ‘next_token’:
lexer.c:23:12: warning: function returns address of local variable [-Wreturn-local-addr]
   23 |     return buf;
      |            ^~~
lexer.c:10:10: note: declared here
   10 |     char buf[64];
      |          ^~~
```

```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include "lexer.h"

/*
 * Returns the next space-separated token from *src and advances *src past it.
 * Returns an empty string at the end of the input.
 */
const char *next_token(const char **src)
{
    char buf[64];
    size_t n = 0;

    while (**src == ' ')
        (*src)++;

    while (**src != '\0' && **src != ' ') {
        if (n < sizeof buf - 1)
            buf[n++] = **src;
        (*src)++;
    }
    buf[n] = '\0';

    return buf;
}
```

That warning describes a real bug, not a style issue. `buf` lives in `next_token`'s stack frame. When the function returns, that memory is released, and the pointer you hand back points at whatever the *next* function call puts there. "Sometimes works" just means nothing has overwritten it yet. It's undefined behavior, and at `-O2` GCC may even replace the returned pointer with `NULL`.

The usual C fix is to let the **caller** own the buffer:

```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include "lexer.h"

/*
 * Copies the next space-separated token from *src into out and advances *src past it.
 * At most outsz-1 characters are stored; longer tokens are truncated. out is always
 * terminated when outsz > 0. Returns the token length, or 0 at the end of the input.
 */
size_t next_token(const char **src, char *out, size_t outsz)
{
    size_t n = 0;

    while (**src == ' ')
        (*src)++;

    while (**src != '\0' && **src != ' ') {
        if (n + 1 < outsz)
            out[n++] = **src;
        (*src)++;
    }
    if (outsz > 0)
        out[n] = '\0';

    return n;
}
```

Usage in `calc.c`:

```c
const char *p = line;
char tok[64];

while (next_token(&p, tok, sizeof tok) > 0) {
    if (!eval_token(&stack, tok)) {
        fprintf(stderr, "unknown token: %s\n", tok);
        return 1;
    }
}
```

Update the prototype in `lexer.h` to match.

Other options, and why I'd avoid them here:

| Option | Problem |
|---|---|
| `static char buf[64];` | Compiles and "works", but every call overwrites the previous token. Two tokens can't be held at once, and it isn't thread-safe. |
| `malloc` + return | Works, but every caller must remember to `free`, for every token. Easy to leak in a loop. |

Also make that warning an error so it can't slip through again. Add `-Werror=return-local-addr` to your flags, or build with `-Wall -Wextra -Werror` while developing. And `-fsanitize=address,undefined` during testing catches this kind of memory bug at the exact line where it happens.

