```cpp
struct Cell {
    int x;
    int y;
};

// ...
std::set<Cell> closed;
// ...
closed.insert(current);   // line 61
```

Yes, that's the important bit. `std::set` is a sorted tree, so it compares elements with `std::less<Cell>`, which calls `a < b`, and `Cell` has no `<`. In C++20 you can let the compiler generate all the comparisons:

```cpp
struct Cell {
    int x;
    int y;

    auto operator<=>(const Cell&) const = default; // also gives you ==
};
```

The defaulted `<=>` compares `x` first and then `y` (in declaration order), which is exactly what a set needs.

For an A* closed set, though, a sorted tree is the slowest of the three options:

**1. Hash set.** O(1) average lookups:

```cpp
#include <cstdint>
#include <functional>
#include <unordered_set>

struct Cell {
    int x;
    int y;
    bool operator==(const Cell&) const = default;
};

struct CellHash {
    std::size_t operator()(const Cell& c) const noexcept {
        auto packed = (std::uint64_t(std::uint32_t(c.x)) << 32) | std::uint32_t(c.y);
        return std::hash<std::uint64_t>{}(packed);
    }
};

std::unordered_set<Cell, CellHash> closed;
```

**2. Flat array.** Use it when the grid has fixed bounds, which it usually does. It's the fastest by far, because it needs no hashing and no allocation per cell:

```cpp
std::vector<std::uint8_t> closed(static_cast<std::size_t>(width) * height, 0);

auto index = [width](Cell c) {
    return static_cast<std::size_t>(c.y) * width + c.x;
};

closed[index(current)] = 1;          // insert
if (closed[index(neighbor)]) { ... } // lookup
```

The same trick works for `gScore` and `cameFrom`: store them as `std::vector<float>` and `std::vector<int>` indexed the same way instead of maps. On a 512×512 grid that's the difference between a search that feels instant and one you can watch.

Your open set will hit the same error if it's a `std::priority_queue<Node>`. There, pass a comparator on the f-score, for example `std::greater<>` with a `Node::operator>` that compares `f`, rather than making `Cell` itself orderable.

A mutex, two condition variables and a `closed` flag. `pop` returns `std::optional<T>`, and an empty optional means "closed and drained". That is the signal the consumer loop needs.

```cpp
#pragma once
#include <condition_variable>
#include <cstddef>
#include <deque>
#include <mutex>
#include <optional>

template <typename T>
class BoundedQueue {
public:
    explicit BoundedQueue(std::size_t capacity) : capacity_(capacity) {}

    BoundedQueue(const BoundedQueue&) = delete;
    BoundedQueue& operator=(const BoundedQueue&) = delete;

    // Blocks while the queue is full. Returns false if the queue has been closed.
    bool push(T value) {
        std::unique_lock lock(mutex_);
        not_full_.wait(lock, [&] { return closed_ || items_.size() < capacity_; });
        if (closed_) return false;
        items_.push_back(std::move(value));
        lock.unlock();
        not_empty_.notify_one();
        return true;
    }

    // Never blocks. Returns false if the queue is full or closed.
    bool try_push(T value) {
        {
            std::lock_guard lock(mutex_);
            if (closed_ || items_.size() >= capacity_) return false;
            items_.push_back(std::move(value));
        }
        not_empty_.notify_one();
        return true;
    }

    // Blocks while the queue is empty. Returns std::nullopt once it is closed and drained.
    std::optional<T> pop() {
        std::unique_lock lock(mutex_);
        not_empty_.wait(lock, [&] { return closed_ || !items_.empty(); });
        if (items_.empty()) return std::nullopt; // closed and nothing left
        T value = std::move(items_.front());
        items_.pop_front();
        lock.unlock();
        not_full_.notify_one();
        return value;
    }

    // Wakes every waiting thread. Items already queued can still be popped.
    void close() {
        {
            std::lock_guard lock(mutex_);
            closed_ = true;
        }
        not_full_.notify_all();
        not_empty_.notify_all();
    }

private:
    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::condition_variable not_full_;
    std::condition_variable not_empty_;
    std::deque<T> items_;
    const std::size_t capacity_;
    bool closed_ = false;
};
```

Wiring it up:

```cpp
BoundedQueue<AudioFrame> frames(64);
std::atomic<std::uint64_t> dropped{0};

std::thread encoder_thread([&] {
    while (auto frame = frames.pop()) {   // ends after close() + drain
        encoder.encode(*frame);
    }
    encoder.flush();
});

// in the capture callback:
if (!frames.try_push(AudioFrame(samples, count))) {
    dropped.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
}

// shutdown, in this order:
capture.stop();        // 1. no more producers
frames.close();        // 2. wake the encoder; it drains what's queued
encoder_thread.join(); // 3. wait for the last frame to be encoded
```

Design notes:

- **The predicate overloads of `wait`** handle spurious wakeups. Never call `wait` without a condition.
- **Notify after unlocking**, so the woken thread doesn't immediately block again on the mutex we still hold.
- **`close()` lets the consumer drain.** After `close()`, `push` fails, but `pop` keeps returning items until the queue is empty. That's the "exit after what's left" you asked for. If you'd rather drop pending frames, clear `items_` inside `close()`.

**On the audio side, use `try_push`, not `push`.** Audio capture callbacks run on a realtime thread with a fixed deadline. If the encoder falls behind and `push` blocks, you get glitches in the capture itself. Dropping a frame and counting it is the lesser evil. Strictly speaking, even `try_push` takes a mutex, and constructing an `AudioFrame` probably allocates. Both are fine for most desktop apps, but for hard realtime use a lock-free single-producer/single-consumer ring of preallocated frames instead.

With exactly one producer (the ISR) and one consumer (the main loop), you don't need to disable interrupts at all, as long as:

1. **each index has exactly one writer.** Only the ISR writes `head`, only the main loop writes `tail`.
2. **the data byte is stored before the index is published.** That's a release store, and the reader pairs it with an acquire load.

C11 atomics express point 2 directly. On Cortex-M, 32-bit atomic loads and stores are plain `ldr`/`str` plus a barrier, with no locking.

```c
/* uart_rb.h */
#ifndef UART_RB_H
#define UART_RB_H

#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <stdint.h>

#define UART_RB_SIZE 256u /* must be a power of two */
_Static_assert((UART_RB_SIZE & (UART_RB_SIZE - 1u)) == 0u, "UART_RB_SIZE must be a power of two");

typedef struct {
    uint8_t data[UART_RB_SIZE];
    _Atomic uint32_t head;     /* total bytes written; written only by the ISR */
    _Atomic uint32_t tail;     /* total bytes read; written only by the main loop */
    volatile uint32_t dropped; /* bytes lost because the buffer was full */
} uart_rb_t;

/* Producer: call only from the ISR. Returns false if the byte had to be dropped. */
static inline bool uart_rb_put(uart_rb_t *rb, uint8_t byte)
{
    uint32_t head = atomic_load_explicit(&rb->head, memory_order_relaxed);
    uint32_t tail = atomic_load_explicit(&rb->tail, memory_order_acquire);

    if (head - tail == UART_RB_SIZE) {
        rb->dropped++;
        return false;
    }
    rb->data[head & (UART_RB_SIZE - 1u)] = byte;
    atomic_store_explicit(&rb->head, head + 1u, memory_order_release);
    return true;
}

/* Consumer: call only from the main loop. Returns false if the buffer is empty. */
static inline bool uart_rb_get(uart_rb_t *rb, uint8_t *out)
{
    uint32_t tail = atomic_load_explicit(&rb->tail, memory_order_relaxed);
    uint32_t head = atomic_load_explicit(&rb->head, memory_order_acquire);

    if (head == tail)
        return false;
    *out = rb->data[tail & (UART_RB_SIZE - 1u)];
    atomic_store_explicit(&rb->tail, tail + 1u, memory_order_release);
    return true;
}

#endif /* UART_RB_H */
```

The ISR and the read function:

```c
/* uart2.c */
#include "stm32l4xx.h"
#include "uart_rb.h"

static uart_rb_t uart2_rx;

void USART2_IRQHandler(void)
{
    uint32_t isr = USART2->ISR;

    if (isr & USART_ISR_RXNE) {
        /* reading RDR also clears RXNE */
        (void)uart_rb_put(&uart2_rx, (uint8_t)USART2->RDR);
    }
    if (isr & USART_ISR_ORE) {
        /* hardware overrun: clear it, otherwise reception can stall */
        USART2->ICR = USART_ICR_ORECF;
    }
}

bool uart2_read_byte(uint8_t *out)
{
    return uart_rb_get(&uart2_rx, out);
}

uint32_t uart2_dropped(void)
{
    return uart2_rx.dropped;
}
```

```c
/* main loop */
uint8_t b;
while (uart2_read_byte(&b)) {
    nmea_feed(b);
}
```

Why this fixes "loses bytes or returns old ones":

- **Free-running counters.** `head` and `tail` just count up and wrap at 2³². `head - tail` is the fill level, even across the wrap, because the size divides 2³². Full and empty are different values (`== SIZE` vs `== 0`), so you don't waste a slot and there's no off-by-one. The classic "old bytes" bug comes from the full and empty states being confused.
- **Release/acquire ordering.** Without it, the compiler may move the `data[...]` store after the `head` update. The main loop then sees the new `head` and reads a stale byte. `volatile` alone doesn't prevent that, because `data` itself isn't volatile.
- **Clearing ORE.** If the main loop is slow once and the UART overruns, the overrun flag stays set and RXNE interrupts stop on many STM32 families. That looks like "lost bytes" forever after.

Sizing: at 115200 baud you get about 11.5 kB/s. 256 bytes covers a main-loop stall of about 22 ms. If your loop can block longer (flash writes, display updates), use 1024. Watch `uart2_dropped()` during testing, and it will tell you whether the size is enough.

The register names are for the L4/F7/G4-style USART. On F1/F4 the flags are in `SR` and the data in `DR`, and ORE is cleared by reading `SR` then `DR`.

Our little game engine has this Scene class. Last week someone copied a Scene by value (passed it to a function without &) and we got a double free crash on exit. Can you refactor it to modern C++17 so this can't happen again? Node.texture is NOT owned by the node, textures belong to the scene.

```cpp
struct Node {
    std::string name;
    Node* parent = nullptr;
    std::vector<Node*> children;
    Texture* texture = nullptr;   // not owned
    explicit Node(std::string n) : name(std::move(n)) {}
};

class Scene {
public:
    Scene() : root(new Node("root")), camera(nullptr) {}

    ~Scene() {
        deleteTree(root);
        delete camera;
        for (size_t i = 0; i < textures.size(); ++i) delete textures[i];
    }

    Node* getRoot() { return root; }

    Node* addNode(Node* parent, const std::string& name) {
        Node* n = new Node(name);
        n->parent = parent;
        parent->children.push_back(n);
        return n;
    }

    void setCamera(Camera* c) {
        delete camera;
        camera = c;
    }

    Camera* getCamera() { return camera; }

    Texture* loadTexture(const std::string& path) {
        Texture* t = new Texture();
        if (!t->load(path)) {
            return nullptr;
        }
        textures.push_back(t);
        return t;
    }

private:
    void deleteTree(Node* n) {
        for (Node* c : n->children) deleteTree(c);
        delete n;
    }

    Node* root;
    Camera* camera;
    std::vector<Texture*> textures;
};
```

The crash comes from the implicitly generated copy constructor. It copies the raw pointers, both Scenes believe they own the same nodes, and both destructors delete them. The fix is to make ownership part of the types: every owning pointer becomes a `std::unique_ptr`, and non-owning pointers stay raw (or become references). Then the compiler deletes the copy operations for you, and the destructor can go away entirely (the "rule of zero").

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <memory>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

struct Node {
    explicit Node(std::string n, Node* p = nullptr) : name(std::move(n)), parent(p) {}

    std::string name;
    Node* parent;                                // non-owning, null for the root
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Node>> children; // owned
    const Texture* texture = nullptr;            // non-owning, the Scene owns textures
};

class Scene {
public:
    Scene() : root_(std::make_unique<Node>("root")) {}

    Node& root() { return *root_; }

    Node& addNode(Node& parent, std::string name) {
        auto& child = parent.children.emplace_back(std::make_unique<Node>(std::move(name), &parent));
        return *child;
    }

    // Destroys node and its whole subtree. node must not be the root.
    void removeNode(Node& node) {
        auto& siblings = node.parent->children;
        siblings.erase(std::find_if(siblings.begin(), siblings.end(),
                                    [&](const auto& p) { return p.get() == &node; }));
    }

    void setCamera(std::unique_ptr<Camera> camera) { camera_ = std::move(camera); }
    Camera* camera() const { return camera_.get(); }

    const Texture* loadTexture(const std::string& path) {
        auto texture = std::make_unique<Texture>();
        if (!texture->load(path)) {
            return nullptr; // unique_ptr frees it; the old code leaked here
        }
        return textures_.emplace_back(std::move(texture)).get();
    }

private:
    std::unique_ptr<Node> root_;
    std::unique_ptr<Camera> camera_;
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Texture>> textures_;
};
```

What you get:

| | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| `Scene b = a;` | compiles, double free at exit | **compile error** (copy constructor is deleted) |
| `Scene b = std::move(a);` | same double free | works; `a` is left empty |
| texture fails to load | leaked | freed automatically |
| destructor | hand-written, recursive | generated; the members clean up in order |

The call sites change a little:

```cpp
Scene scene;
Node& player = scene.addNode(scene.root(), "player");
Node& sword = scene.addNode(player, "sword");
sword.texture = scene.loadTexture("assets/sword.png");
scene.setCamera(std::make_unique<Camera>(60.0f));

void renderScene(const Scene& scene); // must take a reference now, by value won't compile
```

Things to be aware of:

- **Moving a `Scene` keeps node pointers valid.** The nodes live on the heap and only the `unique_ptr`s move, so `parent` pointers and the `Node&` references you hand out stay correct. A `std::vector<Node>` (nodes stored by value) would *not* have that property.
- **Non-owning pointers can still dangle.** If you remove a node, any `Node&` someone kept is invalid, same as before. If that becomes a problem, hand out stable IDs or handles instead of references.
- **Very deep trees** destroy recursively through `unique_ptr`, just like your `deleteTree`. With normal scene depths that's fine. With tens of thousands of levels you'd want an iterative teardown.
- **If you need to copy scenes on purpose** (prefabs, undo snapshots), write an explicit `Scene clone() const` that deep-copies the nodes and decides what to do with the textures. Usually the clone shares them via `std::shared_ptr<Texture>` at that point.

